          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion in the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            (i.e. in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-simplicial+object"} semi-simplicial object
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed (infinity,1)-category
          %p
            Similarly to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-simplicial+sets"} semi-simplicial sets
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                One can define fibered semi-simplicial types as an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-presheaf"} (infinity,1)-presheaf
                on the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_+
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite"} finite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited"} inhabited
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinals"} ordinals
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injections"} injections
                via synthetic
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %li
              %p
                One can define indexed semi-simplicial types as a family of families by iterating the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics+of+dependent+types"} correspondence between fibrations and families
                based on “iterated dependencies”.
          %p
            None of these approaches are currently possible in plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            due to the issue of handling infinity many data in a finitary manner, whether for the iterated families of semi-simplicial types or the coherence laws of
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modalities
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+%28infinity%2C1%29-category+theory"} synthetic (infinity,1)-category theory
                (e.g.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+type+theory"} simplicial type theory
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+type+theory"} triangulated type theory
            %li
              %p
                other levels / non-fibrant types /
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+record+types"} infinitary record types
                (e.g.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-level+type+theory"} two-level type theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory+with+shapes"} type theory with shapes
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+System"} Homotopy Type System
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/explicit+parametricity"} explicit parametricity
                (e.g. some
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/displayed+type+theory"} displayed type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+type+theory"} cohesive type theory
                with
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sufficient+cohesion"} sufficient cohesion
          %p
            Historically, the “iterated dependency” approach to semi-simplicial sets has given dependent type theorists a candidate approach for tackling coherence issues in dependent type theory. With simplicial sets, it’s hard to see how one might tackle or avoid them. (Comparing it to the semi-simplicial approach, one requires degeneracy maps and equations between them, which lets loose the spectre of coherence again.) Furthermore, reasoning about Kan simplicial sets seems to insist on classical logic. For example, the classical result stating the homotopy equivalence of fibers of a Kan fibration cannot be proved constructively (
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/ufias2012/files/countermodel.pdf"}> pdf
            ). In homotopy-theoretic terms, this is because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_+
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+category"} direct category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is not.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#fibered_semisimplicial_types Fibered semi-simplicial types
          %p
            We work in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            which supports
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+%28infinity%2C1%29-category+theory"}
                synthetic
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
